     "existentialism": "A philosophy centered on individual freedom, choice and responsibility. Existentialists hold that people are not given a fixed purpose, and must create meaning and live authentically through their own decisions, without hiding behind roles, crowds or excuses. In politics it favors personal autonomy and is wary of anything that dissolves the individual into a collective.",
     "stoicism": "An ancient philosophy of virtue, self-command and acceptance. Stoics hold that a good life comes from wisdom, courage, justice and self-discipline, and from focusing on what is within one's control while meeting what is not with calm. In politics it appears as an ethic of duty, restraint and civic virtue.",
     "utilitarianism": "An ethical view that judges actions and policies by their consequences for the overall well-being of everyone affected. Utilitarians seek the greatest good for the greatest number and the least suffering, weighing costs and benefits impartially. In politics it supports evidence-based welfare policy and the weighing of trade-offs, and it can justify overriding individual claims for the wider good.",
-    "deontology": "An ethical view that some duties, rights and moral rules bind us whatever the consequences. Deontologists hold that certain acts, such as treating people merely as means, breaking promises or violating basic rights, are wrong even when they would produce better outcomes. In politics it underlies rights-based liberalism, natural law and the rule of law."
+    "deontology": "An ethical view that some duties, rights and moral rules bind us whatever the consequences. Deontologists hold that certain acts, such as treating people merely as means, breaking promises or violating basic rights, are wrong even when they would produce better outcomes. In politics it underlies rights-based liberalism, natural law and the rule of law.",
+    "historical_materialism": "The theory, developed by Marx and Engels, that history is driven by the way people produce what they need. The mode of production and the class relations that grow from it form the base of society, while law, politics, religion and ideas rise on top of it as a superstructure; conflicts between classes push each stage of society into the next. In politics it appears in class analysis, in the expectation that social orders pass through stages, and in the claim that lasting change comes from changing who owns and controls production.",
+    "absolute_idealism": "The view, set out by Hegel and carried on by later idealists, that reality is one rational whole, the Absolute, in which mind comes to know itself through history. Contradictions between opposing ideas are resolved at higher stages, and individuals find real freedom only as members of the ethical communities, above all the state, in which reason takes concrete form. In politics it appears in organic views of society, in ethical conceptions of the state and in the belief that history has a rational direction.",
+    "actual_idealism": "Giovanni Gentile's doctrine that nothing exists apart from thought in the act of thinking: reality is made, moment by moment, by the pure act of the mind, and a thing, a past or a nation exists only as it is thought and willed now. Knowing and willing are one, so thinking is itself a form of action, and the individual spirit realizes itself by joining the shared spirit of the nation and the state. In politics it appears in the cult of action, in the identification of citizen and state and in Italian Fascism's claim to be a spiritual revolution.",
+    "positivism": "Auguste Comte's view that real knowledge comes only from observable facts and the laws science finds in them, with theology and metaphysics left behind as earlier stages of thought. It extends the methods of natural science to society, which can be studied, predicted and managed by experts. In politics it appears in faith in progress, in rule by scientific and administrative competence, in secular civic education and in the motto of order and progress.",
+    "thomism": "The philosophy of Thomas Aquinas, holding that reason and faith agree, that everything has a nature and a purpose, and that human reason can read a natural law that tells people what is good and binds rulers and subjects alike. Human law is just only if it follows from that law, and the common good guides communities from the family up to the state. In politics it appears in Catholic social teaching, in the principle of subsidiarity, in limits on state power under a higher law and in the defense of family, property and a moral order."
 };
 
